WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.477
The browser lab uses five small synthetic tables.

00:00:03.477 --> 00:00:10.407
The admissions table gives us patient admissions, diagnoses, and admission and discharge times.

00:00:10.407 --> 00:00:14.677
The sales table gives us products, quantities, prices, categories, and dates.

00:00:14.677 --> 00:00:19.782
Employees is useful for salary and department examples.

00:00:19.782 --> 00:00:24.317
Product sales is intentionally small so that subtotals from ROLLUP, CUBE,

00:00:24.317 --> 00:00:26.297
and GROUPING SETS are easy to inspect.

00:00:26.297 --> 00:00:32.597
Grades gives us course, semester, grade, and enrollment examples.

00:00:32.597 --> 00:00:35.187
These are teaching datasets, so you can experiment freely.

00:00:35.187 --> 00:00:39.382
The PostgreSQL database runs in the browser and can be reset.

00:00:39.382 --> 00:00:45.962
Do not paste real confidential or production data into the browser editor.

00:00:45.962 --> 00:00:51.882
The goal here is to learn the SQL pattern with safe practice data.

00:00:51.882 --> 00:00:54.672
For this activity, make a prediction before you run anything.

00:00:54.672 --> 00:01:00.042
State what one output row should represent, which records you expect to remain,

00:01:00.042 --> 00:01:05.847
and what edge case is most likely to expose a mistake.

00:01:05.847 --> 00:01:09.997
Then run the SQL and compare the result with that prediction.

00:01:09.997 --> 00:01:17.057
If it does not match, debug the logic in small pieces instead of changing several clauses at once.

00:01:17.057 --> 00:01:21.227
This is the same habit you would use in a real analytics project:

00:01:21.227 --> 00:01:25.933
validate the meaning of the result, not only whether the statement executes.
